          <p>
            Quest Salted Caramel carries 310 mg of sodium per scoop, about double its cookies and cream, and ISO100 Salted Caramel carries 190 mg against 130 mg for its cookies and cream. For most healthy people that is not a problem. The Dietary Guidelines for Americans recommend keeping sodium under 2,300 mg a day for adults, so one scoop of Quest is about 13% of that. Two scoops a day plus a salty diet adds up faster, though, and people with high blood pressure or kidney disease should count it and ask their doctor or dietitian about a sensible limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein moves too. Quest's salted caramel gives 26 g per 34 g scoop, the most of its three flavors we checked, while its cookies and cream gives only 20 g. ISO100 holds 25 g across flavors but drops to 110 calories in salted caramel. Myprotein's isolate is the reminder that market matters: UK salted caramel tubs list about 21 to 22 g per 25 g scoop, while US database entries show about 25 g and roughly 110 calories, so read the panel on the tub you actually receive.
          </p>

          <p>
            If sodium is your main concern, ISO100 Salted Caramel is the lower-sodium choice of the two brands with published panels. Salted caramel is otherwise one of the leaner dessert flavors, because the caramel is flavoring rather than sugar. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide compares isolates on protein per calorie across all flavors.
